little liechtenstein  nope not luxembourg down a bit yes that  small one  lichtenstein or the principality of  liechtenstein  is a small german-speaking country  surrounded  by austria to the east and switzerland  to the west  it is the fourth smallest country in  europe in both land  area and population but  why does this tiny country even exist  and how has it stayed independent for so  long  [Music]  liechtenstein like other microstates in  europe has a reputation for being a rich  tax haven for foreign billionaires  though it has been working to rid itself  of that image  it's a semi-constitutional monarchy  being ruled by the prince of  liechtenstein in concert with parliament  the prince is known to be one of the  most powerful monarchs in europe having  the power to veto legislation  appoint judges and dismiss government  ministers  so how did this small rich and frankly  unusual country come to be well it all  started in the 18th century  in the holy roman empire the holy roman  empire is a pretty complex topic that  historians  still argue about today but basically it  was a political entity made up  of a myriad of small mostly  german-speaking kingdoms  principalities duchies and the like that  were all under the authority of the holy  roman emperor  a position created whole cloth by the  pope in rome  the complexities of the empire aren't  quite in the purview of this video  but you do need to know the basics of  its political system  so the different segments of the empire  if powerful and  important enough would be given  representation in the imperial diet  or reichstag think of it similar to a  senate or  parliament where representatives of  different regions meet to make decisions  of course in this case the  representatives were wealthy land-owning  nobles who earned their positions by  being born into them instead of  democratically elected politicians  but whatever also the diet was not a  legislative body  but more a council of advisors for the  emperor  so back to liechtenstein nope not the  land  but the family the house of luchenstein  was a minor  noble family of the holy roman empire  they got their name from castle  liechtenstein  where they had resided in the middle  ages which is now ironically  in austria anyway the family was rich  and powerful  but they didn't have a seat in the reich  dog you see to earn a seat in the diet  you had to meet two criteria one the  emperor had to grant a seat to you  and two the family had to enjoy what's  called immediacy  what that meant was that the land they  owned had to have no authority above it  save the emperor himself the house of  liechtenstein did indeed  own land but their lands were  technically in the dominions of the  habsburgs  this all gets into the confusing legal  nature of  feudalism and fiefdoms but all you need  to know is that the liechtenstein family  didn't meet the requirements for an  imperial seat  so in the early 1700s the head of the  house of liechtenstein  hans adams the first bought two small  counties called baduts and schellenberg  from another noble family the house of  hohenems  this purchase was all that was needed to  give the liechtenstein family a seat at  the reichdog  and in 1719 emperor charles vi  combined vaduz and schellenberg into the  principality of liechtenstein  naming it after the noble family that  had recently bought it  it's important to understand that the  princes of liechtenstein bought the land  for  political expediency they resided in  vienna  and continued to do so after they became  the rulers of liechtenstein  in fact the family didn't step foot in  the land for about a hundred years after  they bought it  so that's how the country got its start  as a small principality of the holy  roman empire  in 1806 the holy roman empire fell to  french forces led by napoleon bonaparte  napoleon then created the confederation  of the rhine  a replacement state that was under the  thumb of the empire of france  liechtenstein along with many of the  former imperial estates  joined this new confederation but it  fell apart 10 years later and was  replaced with the new  german confederation the german  confederation was  similar to the holy roman empire in that  it was a loose union between the small  german states  but was even less cohesive due to its  having no real ruler  also the two main german powers prussia  and austria didn't want it to become its  own unified country  they had a number of conflicts to see  who would control the german lands  and in 1866 prussia won that debate in  the seven weeks war  prussia then gobbled up all of the other  states one by one  until the german empire was finally  established in 1871  but liechtenstein did not join the new  german empire  it stayed independent even after the  fall of the german confederation  why well there are two main reasons  the first is simple geography i mean  look at europe at this time  austria who had not joined the new  empire had lands  here above liechtenstein if lieutenstein  had joined germany  it would have been a strange little  enclave  the second reason was that liechtenstein  had always been closer politically and  culturally  with austria this makes sense seen as  the princes of liechtenstein were  austrian and still at this point lived  in vienna  so why didn't austria take it over well  honestly there's no answer to that  austria had no interest in annexing it  and lichtenstein had no interest in  merging with austria  so yeah in many ways liechtenstein  managed to stay independent  basically by laying low it didn't fight  to stay independent  it didn't even have an army and still  doesn't  during world war 1 liechtenstein turned  away from austria  who as the austro-hungarian empire was  entrenched in war  and towards switzerland it joined a  monetary and defensive union with the  country  and has been close to it ever since  internationally  liechtenstein may as well be a part of  switzerland and i honestly wouldn't be  surprised if it merged with switzerland  in the future though i wouldn't  necessarily bet on it  the principality of liechtenstein is a  fascinating vestige of europe's  feudal past before the rise of the  nation-state  when most land was ruled by small noble  families  or huge multi-ethnic empires in today's  world it is a strange phenomenon  but seeing now the small nation's  prosperity and success  it shows us that perhaps bigger is not  always better  thank you very much for watching if you  haven't already please subscribe to the  channel  and if you liked the video give it a  thumbs up and leave a comment letting me  know what you think  bye

